Describe the {semicircle|semiT} *ADK* on {AB}, fit {AD} into it equal to {C}, and join {DB}. [!just IV.1]

It is then clear that the angle {ADB|angADB} is right, and that the square on {AB} is greater than the square on {AD}, that is, {C}, by the square on {DB}. [!just III.31; I.47]

Similarly also, *if two straight lines are given, then the straight line the square on which equals the sum of the squares on them is found in this manner.*

Let {AD} and {DB} be the given two straight lines, and let it be required to find the straight line the square on which equals the sum of the squares on them.

Place them so as to contain a right angle {ADB|angADB}, and join {AB}.

It is again manifest that the straight line the square on which equals the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB} is {AB}. [!just I.47]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
